# No check: field — this is the shell itself, always here.
# These are the default emacs-mode bindings, identical in bash and zsh, and
# compiled in rather than configured — so they are literal, not probed.
name:  shell
what:  line editing and job control — works in every terminal, before any tool
docs:  man zshzle, man bash (READLINE)

= MOVING — stop using the left arrow key
Ctrl-a       | jump to the start of the line
Ctrl-e       | jump to the end of the line
Alt-b        | back one word
Alt-f        | forward one word
Ctrl-b       | back one character (same as Left)
Ctrl-f       | forward one character (same as Right)

= DELETING — and getting it back
Ctrl-w       | delete the word before the cursor. The one to know
Ctrl-u       | delete everything before the cursor
Ctrl-k       | delete everything after the cursor
Alt-d        | delete the word after the cursor
Ctrl-y       | paste back whatever you just deleted
Ctrl-_       | undo the last edit (Ctrl-/ on some keyboards)

= HISTORY — you have already typed it once
Ctrl-r       | search backwards. Type to narrow, Ctrl-r again for older hits
Ctrl-g       | abandon the search and keep the line you had
Up / Down    | step through recent commands
Alt-.        | insert the last argument of the previous command

= WHEN IT IS STILL RUNNING
Ctrl-c       | stop it
Ctrl-z       | suspend it and get the prompt back
Ctrl-d       | end of input. On an empty prompt, it logs you out

= COMPLETION — the tab key is not optional
Tab          | complete a command, a path, a flag, a branch name
Tab Tab      | show every candidate when there is more than one
Ctrl-l       | clear the screen without losing the line you are typing
